      "narrative": "Maricopa County\u2019s residential market is flat to slightly down on prices, with the median sale price over the three months ending May 2026 at about $484,000, down 0.81% year over year.[2] Inventory is near balanced: county-wide single-family homes had about 4.0 months of supply in May 2026 and a 98.2% list-price-received ratio, indicating homes generally sell close to asking but not in a frenzy.[6] Overall dynamics are tilted toward buyers at the metro level, with a Cromford-style Overall Market Index around 81 pointing to buyer-favorable conditions driven more by soft demand than by excess inventory, and with concessions and rate buydowns common.[3] A key driver is continued population and migration growth: Maricopa County added roughly 57,000 residents last year, one of the largest gains of any U.S. county, while domestic in-migration into Greater Phoenix has remained strong despite weaker international migration.[3]",
